The \tableofcontents command generates the table of contents automatically based on the sections and subsections in the document. The sections and subsections are created using the \section and \subsection commands, respectively. Each section and subsection is then listed in the table of contents, along with the page number where it can be found.

claude-v1:
 Here is an example of how to automatically generate a table of contents in a LATEX document:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{tocloft} % Allows customization of the table of contents

\usepackage{hyperref} % Allows hyperlinks in the PDF

\usepackage{titletoc} % Allows customization of the table of contents entries

\setcounter{tocdepth}{2} % Levels in the table of contents

\titlecontents{section}[0pt] % Format of section entries
{\addvspace{0.5em}}  
{\contentslabel{0.5em}} 
{}                  
{\titlerule*[0.5pc]{.}\contentspage} 

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents % Prints the table of contents

\section{Section 1} 
\subsection{Subsection 1.1}
\subsection{Subsection 1.2}

\section{Section 2}
\subsection{Subsection 2.1} 

\end{document}
```

This will generate a table of contents with:

- Entries for sections and subsections (due to \setcounter{tocdepth}{2})
- Custom formatting for the section entries (due to \titlecontents) 
- Hyperlinks in the PDF (due to \usepackage{hyperref})
- The \tableofcontents command actually prints the table of contents.

So the output PDF will have a table of contents with links to each section and subsection. Let me know if you have any other questions!

