Here's a LaTeX code to draw a chessboard:
```ruby
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{chessboard}

\begin{document}

\begin{center}
\begin{chessboard}
\end{chessboard}
\end{center}

\end{document}
```
The `graphicx` package is used to include images in LaTeX, while the `chessboard` package provides the `chessboard` environment that draws a chessboard.

Note that the above code will only produce a blank chessboard. To add the FEN (Forsyth–Edwards Notation) of a specific chessboard state, you need to modify the code to include the appropriate commands for placing and moving pieces. Here's an example of how to add a single chess piece on a specific square:
```ruby
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{chessboard}

\begin{document}

\begin{center}
\begin{chessboard}
\piece{R}{1}{1}
\end{chessboard}
\end{center}

\end{document}
```
In this example, the `piece` command is used to place a rook on the square at position (1,1). You can replace `R` with the piece symbol of your choice (e.g., `K` for king, `Q` for queen, `R` for rook, etc.). The first argument specifies the row, the second argument specifies the column, and the third argument specifies the file (a, b, c, or d).
